(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-exam-1-risk-ratios-quiz-version-2"]={"title":"Risk and Ratios Quiz 2 — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Exam 1","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"What is needed to detect a difference between exposed and unexposed groups when the risk is small?","choices":["A larger affected population","A shorter follow-up period","Fewer unexposed persons","A single exposed patient"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, because a small risk produces few extra cases, so many more people must be affected before exposed and unexposed groups can be told apart. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"Incidence is 15 per 500 in exposed persons and 5 per 500 in unexposed persons. What is the attributable risk?","choices":["3.0","0.04","0.03","0.02"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: the exposed incidence is 0.03 and the unexposed incidence is 0.01, and their difference is 0.02. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Which pair of methods is applied only in the design phase?","choices":["Matching and stratification","Randomization and restriction","Simple and multivariable adjustment","Stratification and randomization"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: both are built into the study plan, whereas matching works at either phase and the remaining methods are analysis-phase methods. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"What does a relative risk of 1 indicate?","choices":["Exposed persons have double the risk","Exposure fully protects against disease","Every exposed person develops the disease","Equal risk in exposed and unexposed"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: 1 is the null value, meaning absolute risk in the exposed equals absolute risk in the unexposed, so there is no association between exposure and disease. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Which situation favors a case-control design?","choices":["A common disease with rapid onset","A need to measure incidence directly","A rare disease with long latency","A budget for decades of follow-up"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: a cohort would need a very large group followed for years for few cases, whereas a case-control study starts from the cases and needs a smaller sample. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"A study reports a relative risk of 0.6 for an exposure. What does that mean?","choices":["Exposed persons have 60 percent higher risk","Exposed persons have 40 percent lower risk","Exposed persons have 40 percent higher risk","Exposed and unexposed persons have equal risk"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the risk in exposed persons is 0.6 times that in unexposed persons, which is 40 percent lower, indicating a possibly protective association. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"Effect modification that strengthens the effect of an exposure is called what?","choices":["Synergistic","Antagonistic","Confounding","Systematic"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, and effect modification that weakens the effect of exposure is antagonistic; neither form is a bias. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"How can a frequent exposure that causes disease go unnoticed?","choices":["The exposure was measured too precisely","No exposed versus unexposed comparison","The disease appeared too quickly","The unexposed group was too large"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct, because when most people are exposed the disease may seem ordinary; comparing exposed persons with unexposed persons is what reveals the risk. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"Researchers randomly select 400 people who exercise and 400 who do not, then follow them for ten years. Why is this not a randomized controlled trial?","choices":["Participants were followed over time","Participants were chosen at random","The groups were equal in size","Exposure was not randomly assigned"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: random assignment of the exposure is what defines a randomized trial; here the groups were defined by an exposure people already had, making this a prospective cohort study. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"In a 2x2 table with exposed cases (a), exposed noncases (b), unexposed cases (c) and unexposed noncases (d), what is the odds ratio?","choices":["ac divided by bd","ad divided by bc","ab divided by cd","a/(a+b) divided by c/(c+d)"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: the odds of exposure in cases, a over c, divided by the odds of exposure in controls, b over d, simplifies to ad over bc. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"How is attributable risk calculated?","choices":["Exposed incidence divided by unexposed incidence","Unexposed incidence minus exposed incidence","Exposed incidence minus unexposed incidence","Exposed incidence plus unexposed incidence"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: subtracting the incidence in non-exposed persons from that in exposed persons leaves the incidence attributable to the exposure. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"Why do cohort studies usually use relative risk rather than odds ratio?","choices":["Incidence can be calculated directly","Odds cannot be calculated from cohort data","Cohort studies lack an unexposed group","Odds ratios apply only to randomized trials"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: cohort studies can directly calculate the incidence of disease in the exposed versus non-exposed, so relative risk is usually used rather than the odds ratio. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"Why is a cohort study often impractical for an exposure with a long latency and a rare outcome?","choices":["Exposure cannot be measured in them","Latency means long waits, large samples","They cannot follow persons over time","They always rely on recall of exposure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: with a latency between exposure and pathological effect a large sample is needed and only a limited number get the condition; case-control studies need smaller samples and no waiting. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 29"},{"q":"In an individual patient, what is the best predictor of disease?","choices":["Presence of a single risk factor","The relative risk of the exposure","Incidence in the wider population","Presence of early clinical disease"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: risk factors raise the probability of disease, but in an individual it is early clinical disease itself that predicts best. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"In a study of coffee drinking and pancreatic cancer, smoking is more common among coffee drinkers and also raises cancer risk. What role does smoking play?","choices":["An effect modifier","A confounder","The outcome","The exposure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: smoking is related to both the exposure, coffee, and the outcome, cancer, so it can produce a false association, a systematic error. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"Why is a prospective cohort study impractical for rare diseases?","choices":["Enrollment must far exceed the few events","Rare diseases have no measurable exposures","The cohort cannot be followed forward","Standardized measures are unavailable"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: it is impractical because many more subjects must be enrolled than experience the event of interest, and it is also expensive and slow. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 16"},{"q":"The incidence is 0.02 in unexposed persons. An exposure carries a relative risk of 4. What is the incidence in exposed persons?","choices":["0.08","0.06","0.005","0.02"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: relative risk is the exposed incidence divided by the unexposed incidence, so the exposed incidence is 4 times 0.02, which is 0.08. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"How does effect modification differ from confounding?","choices":["It is a real effect, not a bias","It is a bias that must be removed","It arises only in randomized trials","It always weakens the effect"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: confounding is a systematic error to be controlled, whereas effect modification is not a bias but a genuine change in the effect of exposure. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 28"},{"q":"In a cohort, 10 of 500 vaccinated and 40 of 500 unvaccinated persons developed influenza. What is the relative risk of influenza with vaccination?","choices":["4.0","0.06","0.25","0.02"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: 0.02 divided by 0.08 is 0.25; a value below 1 means the vaccinated have lower risk, here one quarter of the risk in the unvaccinated. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 20"},{"q":"On what are probabilities of a future event based when predicting from risk factors?","choices":["One patient followed in detail","Data from groups of people","The clinician's judgment alone","Laboratory results from relatives"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: predictions from risk factors are probabilities drawn from groups, which is why they fit an individual only loosely. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 6"},{"q":"Two exposures each have a relative risk of 2.0. Baseline incidence is 1 in 1,000 for the first and 1 in 10 for the second. Which has the greater attributable risk?","choices":["The first, with the lower baseline","Both, because the relative risks are equal","Neither can be calculated","The second, with the higher baseline"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: attributable risk is the difference in incidence, 0.1 for the second exposure (0.2 minus 0.1) against 0.001 for the first, so the same relative risk hides a huge gap in excess cases. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"What kind of error does confounding cause?","choices":["Error caused by chance alone","Sampling error from a small study","Error from participants dropping out","Systematic error (bias)"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct: confounding causes systematic error, because an additional factor related to both exposure and outcome distorts the association in a consistent way. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 26"},{"q":"A cohort study finds a relative risk of 2.5. How likely are exposed persons to develop the disease compared with unexposed persons?","choices":["2.5 percent more likely","Half as likely","2.5 times as likely","25 percent more likely"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: relative risk is the ratio of the incidence in exposed persons to the incidence in unexposed persons, so 2.5 means exposed persons are two and a half times as likely. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"During two years, 18 of 600 patients on a surgical ward developed a pressure injury. What is the absolute risk?","choices":["3 percent","18 percent","0.3 percent","33 percent"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: absolute risk is incidence, the number of new cases divided by the number of people in the group, so 18 divided by 600 is 0.03. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"How can a long latency between exposure and disease affect the results of a study?","choices":["It makes the association easier to see","It strengthens the association","It has no effect on the results","It can obscure the association"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct, because the disease appears long after the exposure, so the two can look unrelated unless people are followed for a sufficient period of time. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 4"},{"q":"In a case-control study, 20 of 100 cases and 40 of 100 controls had been exposed. What is the odds ratio?","choices":["2.7","0.38","0.50","0.25"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: (20 over 80) divided by (40 over 60) is 0.25 over 0.67, about 0.375, which is below 1, so exposure is associated with lower odds of disease. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"For an intervention that prevents disease, what relative risk is expected?","choices":["Greater than one","Exactly one","Less than one","A negative number"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, because prevention should leave the exposed group, those receiving the intervention, with lower risk than the unexposed group. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"Why does a relative risk alone not show the size of a patient's risk?","choices":["It counts only the exposed group","It cannot exceed two","It cancels out incidence","It is always close to one"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: dividing one incidence by another removes the incidence itself, so a doubling of risk looks the same whether the baseline is 1 in 10,000 or 1 in 10. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Patients with lung disease had six times the odds of prior asbestos exposure compared with patients without it. Which statement follows?","choices":["Six percent of exposed persons develop disease","Unexposed persons have six times the odds of disease","Exposed persons have six times the odds of disease","One in six exposed persons has the disease"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: the odds ratio is the same whichever way it is read, so it can be stated as the odds of disease in the exposed, which is what is actually wanted. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"Why are experimental studies of risk in humans limited?","choices":["Ethical constraints on exposing people","Risk cannot be expressed as a ratio","Experiments cannot measure incidence","Exposures cannot be measured in humans"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct, since investigators cannot ethically assign people to a harmful exposure, so observational designs are used: concurrent (prospective) cohort, historical (retrospective) cohort and case-control studies. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 8"}]};
